The macro has a problem: `const fn first()` — I can't reference `$first_variant` generically. Let me fix the macro: Default for the enum should be the first variant. I can generate it directly in the macro:

```rust
impl Default for $name {
    fn default() -> Self { $name::$first }
}
```
where `$first` is the first variant name — I need to extract it. In macro_rules, I can match the first variant: `($(#[$meta:meta])* pub enum $name:ident { $first:ident = $first_val:literal $(, $($rest:ident = $rest_val:literal),*)? $(,)? })`. Hmm, that's messy with optional trailing comma. Let me restructure:

```rust
macro_rules! proto_str_enum {
    ($(#[$meta:meta])* pub enum $name:ident { $($var:ident = $val:literal),+ $(,)? }) => {
        ... variants ...
        impl Default for $name { fn default() -> Self { Self::FIRST } }
        impl $name { pub const FIRST: Self = $name::first_variant(); ... }
    }
}
```
Still need the first variant name. Use a helper: since all enums' first member is the "0" member, but I can't name it generically... 

Option: add `#[default]`-like info via separate attribute? Simpler: make the macro take the first variant explicitly as part of the invocation:

```rust
proto_str_enum! {
    pub enum ItemType {
        NONE = "ITEM_TYPE_NONE",
        VIRTUAL = "ITEM_TYPE_VIRTUAL",
        ...
    }
}
```
and inside the macro, default = first listed. To get the first listed name in macro_rules, I can do:

```rust
macro_rules! proto_str_enum {
    ($(#[$meta:meta])* pub enum $name:ident { $first:ident = $first_val:literal, $($rest:ident = $rest_val:literal),* $(,)? }) => {
        $(#[$meta])*
        #[derive(...)]
        pub enum $name { $first = $first_val, $($rest = $rest_val),* }
        impl Default for $name { fn default() -> Self { Self::$first } }
        ...
    };
}
```
The `$(,)?` trailing comma: pattern `$first:ident = $first_val:literal, $($rest...),* $(,)?` — handles "A = \"x\", B = \"y\"" and "A = \"x\", B = \"y\"," and "A = \"x\"," and "A = \"x\""? For single variant "A = \"x\"" without comma: `$first = $first_val,` requires the comma... I need to allow single variant without trailing comma. Pattern: `$first:ident = $first_val:literal $(, $($rest:ident = $rest_val:literal),*)? $(,)?` — this allows "A = \"x\"" (no comma), "A = \"x\", B = \"y\"", and trailing comma.

All my enums have ≥2 variants, but be safe.

Also the enum discriminant: `$first = $first_val` — assigning string literals as discriminants is NOT allowed in Rust enums! String discriminants don't exist. So the enum variants should be unit variants WITHOUT discriminants, and the string mapping lives in from_str_name/str_name. Let me fix: `pub enum $name { $first, $($rest),* }` and mapping `$first => $first_val` etc.

